direction: down

title: |md
  ## RCU vs SRCU and where sleepable BPF lives
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

rcu: "Standard RCU\n(Read-Copy Update)" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  rcu_body: |md
    Read side:  
    - rcu_read_lock()  
    - rcu_read_unlock()  
    - **must not sleep** between them  
    - readers don't block the writer  

    **Why no sleep?**  
    Sleeping while in read-side critical  
    section = grace period extends  
    indefinitely = unbounded memory  
    pressure on writers  

    Used in: most kernel data structures,  
    most BPF programs (non-sleepable)  
  |
}

srcu: "SRCU\n(Sleepable RCU)" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  srcu_body: |md
    Read side:  
    - srcu_read_lock()  
    - srcu_read_unlock()  
    - **may sleep** between them  
    - per-domain (each subsystem has own)  

    **Cost:**  
    - Slightly more expensive locks  
    - Bounded memory pressure per-domain  

    Used in: sleepable BPF, some  
    filesystem code, some networking  
  |
}

bpf: "Sleepable BPF programs\nuse a dedicated SRCU domain" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  bpf_body: |md
    - SEC("fentry.s/...")  
    - SEC("lsm.s/...")  
    - SEC("iter.s/...")  

    Trampoline calls:  
    1. srcu_read_lock(&bpf_srcu)  
    2. invoke BPF program (may sleep)  
    3. srcu_read_unlock(&bpf_srcu)  

    Cleanup of detached programs  
    waits for an SRCU grace period.  
  |
}

rcu -> srcu: {style.opacity: 0}
rcu -> bpf: "non-sleepable\nuses RCU"
srcu -> bpf: "sleepable\nuses SRCU"
